it's now been more than a quarter of a  century since Daniel Stern played Marvin  home alone yet it remains his most  recognizable role despite the fact that  he's never stopped acting Stern's stayed  busy so why don't we see him as often  anymore Stern was the narrator on The  Wonder Years between 1988 and 1993 and  he directed a handful of episodes along  the way soon enough he decided he wanted  to take a shot at directing a movie as  well so in 1993 he helmed what would be  his first and last feature the family  baseball comedy Rookie of the Year it's  the story of a 12 year old pitcher who  leads the Chicago Cubs to glory after  discovering that his recently broken arm  has healed with super-strength that's  the sort of logic that you don't think  to question as a kid but the grown-up  critics reviewing the film weren't as  easily impressed despite the poor  reviews the film was a decent box-office  success  racking up over fifty million dollars in  ticket sales stern who also acted in the  movie as pitching coach Phil brickma  went on to direct a few TV episodes here  and there afterwards but Hollywood has  yet to call him back in from the bullpen  the box-office success of the home alone  movies meant that Stern had little  choice but to carry on as a goofball in  other films he reprised his city  slickers role in the 1994 sequel The  Legend of Curly's gold and he handed up  in forgotten comedies like bushwhack and  Celtic pride he then decided to take  radical action to shake off his  typecasting as he signed on for the  controversial 1998 comedy very bad  things the film is about a group of men  on a bachelor weekend trying to dispose  of a prostitute one of them accidentally  killed it was supposed to be edgy but  for the majority of critics it crossed  the line into bad taste far too often  Roger Ebert for one called it  reprehensible in his one-star review  Stern however did think it was funny  telling the a/v Club in a 2014 interview  that he enjoyed it thoroughly but the  gamble didn't exactly pay off for his  career  [Music]  after very bad things Stern's next  credit was a little-known crime caper  called viva los nowhere which failed to  open any new doors for him from the  outside it looked as though his time was  up but while his on-screen career was  floundering he was prospering behind the  scenes as a voice artist Stern did  everything from Burger King and 7up  commercials to Emmy winning shows he  found quite the good fit as the title  character in the TV adaptation of the  Dilbert comic strip between 1999 and  2000 his other notable voice credits  include the likes of Hey Arnold and  Family Guy perhaps the biggest reason  most of us haven't seen Daniel Stern in  a long time is that he went through a  six-year stretch without appearing in  any feature films his last credit before  2018 came in 2012 when he returned to  the realm of festive comedy with a  Christmas story - but this was no home  alone however far from it in fact a  straight-to-dvd sequel to a much-loved  Yuletide classic A Christmas Story 2 was  released just days before a musical  version of the original was set to debut  on Broadway Warner Brothers was likely  attempting to cash in on the hype  surrounding the stage production  unfortunately hardly anyone noticed and  the few critics that bothered to review  the film were less than enthusiastic  about it in 2018 Stern made a not so  glorious return to the screen in the  Netflix original game over man starring  the workaholics guys as hotel  housekeepers who find themselves in a  die hard scenario the reviews were  pretty much just as dreadful as they  were for a Christmas story - I'll make  it up let me just get my head together  and we'll talk about it all right  Stern might be proud of his own career  accomplishments but that's nothing  compared to what he felt after helping  his son become the youngest California  state senator in history in 2016 the  then 34 year old Henry Stern won the  honor of representing the 27th district  which stretches from Malibu to the Santa  Clarita Valley a Democrat the younger  Stern is the first millennial to make it  to the California Senate and he plans on  representing his age group well during  his term one of Henry's biggest  supporters on the trail was his father  who had regularly turned up to support  him in campaign events and endorse him  via social media  when Henry won the elder stern could  barely contain his joy revealing that he  cried when the children's choir began  singing at the swearing  and ceremony in an Instagram post he  wrote I have done all I can for my own  children and they are children no more  [Music]
 

